4. Before entering into the merits of the case, it is pertinent to see why and how section 19A was introduced in the Arms Act. Indian Arms Act, shortly the Act was promulgated in 1878. Thereafter, to meet the call of the day, certain amendments, particularly in the then Bengal and thereafter, in Bangladesh, were made. Originally section 19 was in the Act with a sentence of 3 years with fine. But, since there was offences which require a heavier punishment as there was killing of Europeans and, particularly Hindu flatterers of British Government, and threat of life was there on every movement, section 19A was introduced in the Act as well as certain changes were made in other sections of the original Act through 'The Bengal Criminal Law (Arms and Explosives) Act, 1932', Bengal Act XXI of 1932 on 12-1-1932 and the preamble of this Act was as follows: 

 "An Act to provide enhanced punishment for certain offences under the Indian Arms Act, 1878, and the Explosive Substances Act, 1908, in their application to Bengal and to make special provision for the trial of certain offences under the Indian Arms Act, 1878. 

 WHEREAS it is expedient to provide enhanced punishment for certain offences under the Indian Arms Act 1878, and the Explosive Substances Act, 1908, in their application to Bengal and for this purpose to amend the said Acts in the manner hereinafter appearing; And WHEREAS it is also expedient to make special provision for the trial of certain offences under the Indian Arms Act, 1878; 

 And WHEREAS the previous sanction of the Governor General has been obtained, under sub-section (3) of section 80A of the Government of India Act, to the passing of this Act; 

 1. (I) This Act may be called the Bengal Criminal Law (Arms and Explosives) Act, 1932. 

 5. Section 19A was further amended in 1934 through 'The Bengal Criminal Law Amendment Act, 1934', Bengal Act VII of 1934. This Act was promulgated on 29-3-1934. This time only some minor changes have been made in section 19A. The preamble of this new Act and the changes made in section 19A are quoted for ready reference: 

 "An Act to supplement the criminal law in Bengal. 

 WHEREAS it is expedient to supplement the criminal law in Bengal for the purpose of dealing more effectively with the terrorist movement and to that end to amend the Indian Arms Act, 1878, the Explosive Substances Act, 1908, and the Indian Press (Emergency Powers) Act, 1931, in their application to Bengal, and also to amend the Bengal Criminal Law Amendment Act, 1925, the Bengal Criminal Law Amendment Act, 1930, and the Bengal Suppression of Terrorist Outrages Act, 1932, in the manner hereinafter appearing ; 

 And WHEREAS the previous sanction of the Governor General has been obtained under sub-section (3) of section 80A of the Government of India Act to the passing of this Act; 

 1. This Act may be called the Bengal Criminal Law Amendment Act, 1934. 

 Now section 19 and 19A of the Act both have a punishment up to imprisonment for life. Section 19A was introduced in 1932 for heavier punishment and transportation for life was the punishment as in the then section 19 was with sentence of 3 years. But since section 19 now has a sentence of imprisonment for life then what is the utility for continuing with section 19A. There is yet a little distinction between 19 and 19A. An offence under section 19 is relating to each of the clauses mentioned therein attracting sections 5, 6, 10, 13 to 17 of the Act but an offence of section 19A attracts the clauses 19 (a), (c), (e) or (t) of the Act together as such an offence under section 19A although attracts clauses of section 19 but section 19 does not attract section 19A. Investigating officers or even the Judges made mistake in mentioning the section in the report and also in the judgment. Section 19A, it appears, was introduced to punish the terrorist. But the time has now changed and application of section 19A after the amendment of section 19 in 1991 as aforesaid has become redundant. Now section 19 itself provides for same punishment as of section 19A, as such, to avoid anomaly, in police report or in framing charge, in our opinion, section 19A should go. The law-makers are there to look into the matter.
